{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>But he refused, and said to his master's wife, 'Look, my master.'</b> At the start of his speech he said to her, 'Look, my master,' that is, 'But my master is available to you, so what are you doing to me?' And the reason in the word וימאן ('he refused') indicates the prohibition of the matter and that it was impossible for him, for מאן בו means refusal in the extreme, since through the reasons in the Torah we understand what was not written in it, like the case of the gestures a person makes, through which we know the intention of his heart." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'Look, my master' — he saw the image of Jacob his father, and it said to him: 'Joseph, your brothers are destined to be inscribed on the stones of the ephod; do you wish to be missing from them and become a shepherd of whores?'" "<b>And she said, 'Lie with me.'</b> She spoke in a manner of wantonness and brazenness, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 7:10): ״והנה אשה לקראתו שית זונה ונצורת לב וגו״ — 'And behold, a woman to meet him, dressed as a harlot and guarded of heart, etc.' And this is the talk of an adulterous woman, but a virtuous woman, such as Ruth, said in clean language (Ruth 3:9): ״ופרשת כנפיך על אמתך״ — 'And spread your wings over your handmaid.'" "<b>And he said to his master's wife.</b> It could have said ויאמר לה, 'and he said to her,' but it added to say אשת אדוניו ('his master's wife') to clarify that despite her being the mistress, his master's wife, and he feared her — HaShem he feared more."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:53.173518124Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}